/**
 * @mainpage
 *
 * @section intro Introduction
 *
 * This tool automates the assignment of paper submissions to reviewers
 * for a scientific conference. Each submission has a primary (and optionally
 * secondary) topic domain, and each reviewer has a primary (and optionally
 * secondary) domain of expertise. The tool formulates the assignment as a
 * **Maximum Flow problem** and solves it using either the
 * **Edmonds-Karp** or **Ford-Fulkerson** algorithm.
 *
 *
 * 
 *
 * The tool supports:
 * - Interactive menu mode for manual operation
 * - Batch mode for scripted/automated execution
 * - Risk analysis to identify critical reviewers whose absence breaks the assignment
 *
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section tutorial Getting Started
 *
 * @subsection build Building the Project
 * Build the executable manually as:
 * @code
 * g++ -Wall -ICode/include Code/src/*.cpp -o main
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection interactive Interactive Mode
 * Run the program with no arguments to open the menu:
 * @code
 * ./main
 * @endcode
 * You will be presented with a menu. Select option 1 to load a CSV file,
 * then use the other options to view data, run the assignment and risk analysis.
 *
 * @subsection batch Batch Mode
 * Run with the `-b` flag followed by the input CSV.
 * A third argument (risk file path) is accepted by `main`, but risk output is
 * controlled by the `RiskAnalysis` value in the input file control section.
 * @code
 * ./main -b input.csv [risk.csv]
 * @endcode
 * - input.csv  — the input data file (required)
 * - risk.csv   — optional third argument accepted by batch mode
 *
 * @subsection script Using the Batch Script
 * The project provides a helper script that compiles and runs batch mode:
 * @code
 * chmod +x run_batch.sh
 * ./run_batch.sh input.csv [risk.csv]
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection tests Running Automated Tests
 * Use the test script to compile and run all datasets in `tests/input`:
 * @code
 * chmod +x run_tests.sh
 * ./run_tests.sh
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection input Input File Format
 * The input file must have a .csv extension and follow this structure:
 * @code
 * #Submissions
 * #Id, Title, Authors, E-mail, Primary, Secondary
 * 31, "Paper Title", Author Name, email@domain.com, 3, 4
 * #
 * #Reviewers
 * #Id, Name, E-mail, Primary, Secondary
 * 1, Reviewer Name, reviewer@domain.com, 1,
 * #Parameters
 * MinReviewsPerSubmission, 2
 * MaxReviewsPerReviewer, 4
 * #Control
 * GenerateAssignments, 1
 * RiskAnalysis, 0
 * OutputFileName, "output.csv"
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section contents Documentation Contents
 *
 * - @ref ConferenceData   — Core data structures (Submission, Reviewer, Parameters, Control)
 * - @ref Parser           — CSV input file parser
 * - @ref ConferenceGraph  — Flow network construction
 * - @ref AssignmentSolver — Assignment solver and risk analysis
 * - @ref EdmondsKarp.h      — Edmonds-Karp algorithm (BFS-based Max-Flow)
 * - @ref FordFulkerson.h    — Ford-Fulkerson algorithm (DFS-based Max-Flow)
 * - @ref Menu             — Interactive command-line menu
 * - @ref Graph            — Generic graph data structure (Vertex, Edge, Graph)
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section complexity Main Time Complexities
 *
 * | Function                              | Time Complexity |
 * |---------------------------------------|-----------------|
 * | Parser::parseFile()                   | O(C + N + M)    |
 * | Parser::validate()                    | O(N + M)        |
 * | ConferenceGraph::buildGraph()         | O(N * M)        |
 * | AssignmentSolver::solve() [EK]        | O(V * E^2)      |
 * | AssignmentSolver::solve() [FF]        | O(E * F)        |
 * | AssignmentSolver::exportAssignments() | O(E + A log A)  |
 * | AssignmentSolver::riskAnalysis()      | O(M * V * E^2)  |
 *
 * Where:
 * - C = total input size (characters)
 * - N = number of submissions
 * - M = number of reviewers
 * - V = N + M + 2 (total vertices in flow network)
 * - E = number of edges (O(N*M) worst case)
 * - A = number of assignments made
 * - F = maximum flow value
 *
 * @section extra_docs Extra Documentation
 *
 * - @ref formulations "Algorithm Formulations"
 *   - @ref basic_formulation   "T2.1 - Basic Formulation (Primary Domains Only)"
 *   - @ref risk_analysis_1     "T2.2 - Risk Analysis K = 1"
 *   - @ref risk_analysis_k     "T2.3 - Risk Analysis K > 1"
 *   - @ref general_formulation "T2.4 - General Formulation"
 */